# Copy to .env.local (gitignored) for local dev, and set the same two as
# build-time environment variables on the host (Cloudflare Pages → Settings →
# Environment variables). See DEPLOY.md.
#
# Both are baked into the client bundle at build time — expected and safe for
# these two values, and only these two. The publishable/anon key is designed
# to ship to browsers; it is not a secret. RLS is what actually protects the
# data, which is why DEPLOY.md's two-account check is a release gate rather
# than a nice-to-have.
#
# NEVER put the service_role / "Secret" key in here or anywhere in this repo —
# it bypasses RLS entirely.

VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=<publishable-or-anon-key>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL — dev auto sign-in. Leave both unset unless you're testing the PWA
# on a phone over the LAN; see README "Skipping the magic link locally".
#
# When both are set AND the page is served from a local/private-LAN hostname,
# AuthGate signs in with this password account instead of emailing a magic
# link. It is a REAL session — RLS, sync and the outbox all behave normally.
#
# NO `VITE_` PREFIX, deliberately: Vite only auto-inlines VITE_-prefixed vars,
# so these can never reach a bundle on their own. They're injected by an
# explicit `define` in vite.config.ts that only runs for the dev server and
# `npm run build:local`. Plain `npm run build` — the artifact `wrangler deploy`
# ships — therefore cannot carry them, whatever is set here. Do not add the
# prefix back, and do not set these in the Cloudflare build environment.
#
# `npm run build:local` output has these credentials in plaintext. Never deploy
# it; the build prints a warning to that effect.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DEV_AUTH_EMAIL=dev@example.com
# DEV_AUTH_PASSWORD=
